<!--
  ================================================================================================
  dataservices-service.slnx : the per service solution for the DataServices service.

  PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition, Phase 1. DataServices owns the DataWindow retrieval,
  validation and update triple, the 22 event DataWindow chain and the column expression engine,
  ported from ws_objects/pfw.datawindow.services.pbl.src/ (13 objects, zero native bindings, pure
  PowerScript).

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS LOAD BEARING (constraints C-I and C-L)

  The mandated per service command runs from THIS directory and names no project and no solution.
  Chained with &&, its four invocations are: `cd services/dataservices-service`, `dotnet restore`,
  `dotnet build -c Release`, and `dotnet test` collecting the "XPlat Code Coverage" data collector.

  Every one of those resolves its target by scanning this directory for a solution. They therefore
  work only because exactly ONE solution file lives here. Measured directly on SDK 10.0.302: with a
  second solution file present, the bare commands do not pick one, they fail outright with

      MSBUILD : error MSB1011: Specify which project or solution file to use because this folder
      contains more than one project or solution file.

  The single file rule is therefore the mechanism, not a style preference. Never add a second
  solution file to this directory, and keep this file's name aligned with the directory name.

  FORMAT: .slnx ONLY. NO .sln, AND NO .slnf.

  .NET 10 tooling emits .slnx, the XML solution format, which is what this file is. The shape below
  was established empirically against this SDK before the file was written: `dotnet new sln` emits a
  bare `<Solution>` root, and `dotnet sln add` emits `<Project Path="..." />` children using forward
  slash separators relative to this file's own directory. Legacy solution filters (.slnf) are
  recorded as incompatible with the .slnx format, failing with MSB4014 during restore, build and
  test. Do not reach for one regardless: a per service solution already scoped to two projects has
  nothing left to filter, and every additional solution artifact courts the MSB1011 ambiguity above.

  SCOPE: EXACTLY TWO PROJECTS, AND NOTHING ELSE (constraints C-A and C-D)

  Constraint C-A decomposes rather than collapses, so this solution is service scoped. It must never
  name a project under services/gateway-service/, services/persistence-service/ or
  services/security-service/. The six shared projects are absent on purpose as well, and they are
  not missing: they arrive transitively through the ProjectReference set of the application project
  below, so restore and build already pull the entire closure, including
  shared/PowerFramework.Contracts, which is the sole permitted cross service coupling. Listing them
  here would quietly turn a service scoped solution into a partial whole repository one and forfeit
  the C-A acceptance condition, namely that this service restores, builds and tests from a clean
  checkout without reference to any other service's project. The root PowerFramework.slnx enumerates
  these same two projects for whole repository builds; that overlap is intentional and neither file
  supersedes the other.

  Constraint C-D defers DesignSystem, Documents, Integration and ScriptBridge in full, so no entry
  here names or implies any of them, and there is no placeholder project of any kind.

  ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THAT SCOPING, MEASURED AND DELIBERATELY ACCEPTED

  Because the six shared projects are reached only by ProjectReference and are absent from this
  solution, a solution build does not pass its configuration down to them. MSBuild unsets the parent
  configuration for any referenced project it cannot find in the solution configuration table, so
  during `dotnet build -c Release` here the two projects listed below build Release while the six
  shared projects build in their own default, Debug, and it is those Debug assemblies that get copied
  into the application's Release output. Verified on SDK 10.0.302 by comparing assembly hashes.

  This is accepted rather than worked around, on three grounds. It is inherent to a service scoped
  solution, so the only "fix" would be to list the shared projects here, which is exactly what
  constraint C-A forbids. The mandated command is unaffected: it still reports a clean Release build
  with zero warnings and zero errors, the tests still run, and the coverage report is still produced.
  And the shipped artifact is unaffected, because the container image is produced by
  `dotnet publish -c Release` against the application project rather than by a solution build, which
  was measured to build all six shared projects in Release. Do not "correct" this by adding projects
  to this file.

  THE TEST PROJECT ENTRY IS MANDATORY (constraint C-H)

  `dotnet test` in this directory discovers tests through this solution. Drop the second entry and it
  finds nothing to run, coverlet never emits TestResults/coverage.cobertura.xml, and the 80 percent
  line coverage per service gate cannot be evaluated at all. That failure mode is worse than a broken
  build, because it presents as a green run with a silently unmeasurable gate.

  ENTRY ORDER

  Application before tests, mirroring the dependency direction. Order carries no build semantics,
  since MSBuild derives the graph from ProjectReference rather than from list position. Noted only so
  a later reader is not surprised: regenerating this file with `dotnet sln add` sorts entries
  alphabetically and would place the .Tests entry first. That is harmless, and the ordering below is
  the intentional one.

  INHERITED CONFIGURATION, DELIBERATELY NOT DUPLICATED

  global.json (SDK 10.0.302, rollForward latestFeature), Directory.Build.props (net10.0, nullable
  reference types, implicit usings, warnings promoted to errors, central package management) and
  Directory.Packages.props (every version pin, including the two mandatory security pins) all live at
  the repository root and reach this service by MSBuild directory walk up. No per service copy of any
  of them exists or should be created, which is precisely why every PackageReference in this service
  is versionless.

  LEGACY ANALOGUE, FOR CONTEXT AND NOT FOR TRANSLATION

  The PowerBuilder counterpart of this file is the workspace and target pair: pfw.pbw declares the
  targets, and pfw.pbt declares a single ordered LibList whose ORDER determines symbol resolution
  across one flat global namespace. There is no LibList analogue in .slnx and none is needed, because
  the build graph comes from explicit project references instead of list position. The two
  PowerBuilder build definitions ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/project.srj and p_pfw.srj are reference for
  build INTENT only: they contradict each other (29 versus 28 PBD lines, and a different COM vendor),
  and both name pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which exists nowhere in the repository, so neither would
  build as written. The legacy tree remains read only and is never an edit target (constraint C-C).
  ================================================================================================
-->
<Solution>
  <!--
    The DataServices application. Hosts the gRPC surface for contracts C-03 (DataWindowService) and
    C-04 (ColumnExpressionService) on port 5102, plus the thin REST projection consumed only by
    Gateway, the anonymous /health endpoint and the JWT protected /v1/ping endpoint. Its six shared
    ProjectReference entries pull the remainder of the build closure in behind it.
  -->
  <Project Path="PowerFramework.DataServices/PowerFramework.DataServices.csproj" />

  <!--
    The xunit.v3 unit and service level test project (OutputType Exe, as xunit.v3 requires). It
    references the application project above, and its coverlet.collector reference is what turns the
    mandated `dotnet test` run into the Cobertura report that the coverage gate is measured from.
  -->
  <Project Path="PowerFramework.DataServices.Tests/PowerFramework.DataServices.Tests.csproj" />
</Solution>
